<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-I-3D_geometry" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Geometry of Space</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> Up to this point, we have been working on the flat, two-dimensional canvas of the 
    Cartesian plane (<m>xy</m>-plane). But the physical world we live, move, and build things 
    in is inherently three-dimensional. To model satellite orbits, design computer graphics, 
    analyze fluid dynamics, or navigate physical space, we must expand our mathematical toolkit 
    from two dimensions to three. </p> 
    <image source="vector_1.jpeg" width="30%"/>
    <p>
      In the figure above, we see a point P mapped in three-dimensional space.
    </p>
  </introduction>

